<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
      Root of the mobile theme tree.

      DayNight is what makes values-night/colors.xml resolve at all: with a
      .Light. parent the night resources are never selected, so having the files
      is not the same as having dark mode. Selection follows the system, which is
      AppCompat's default (MODE_NIGHT_FOLLOW_SYSTEM) - no code change needed.

      Android's force-dark transformation stays OFF. Dark mode here means the
      semantic roles resolving their dark values, not the framework inverting
      colours it does not understand.

      Android TV does NOT descend from this. TvTheme spells out its own Light
      parent precisely so this tree can become DayNight without reaching TV.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Myata.Base" parent="Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <!-- The eleven approved v1 semantic roles from A2, wired to the Material
             attributes Phase B will read. Nothing consumes these yet: AppTheme and
             AppTheme0-9 still pin their own colours, and every screen still names
             its colours directly. Phase B removes those overrides screen by screen,
             at which point this mapping starts doing the work. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/primary</item>
        <item name="colorOnPrimary">@color/on_primary</item>
        <item name="colorSurface">@color/surface</item>
        <item name="colorOnSurface">@color/text_primary</item>
        <item name="android:colorBackground">@color/background</item>
        <item name="colorOnBackground">@color/text_primary</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      Android TV theme. A verbatim copy of AppTheme as it stood before the 3.6.6
      mobile migration, with the colours pointed at colors_tv.xml so a later edit
      to the mobile palette cannot change TV.

      The parent is written out rather than inherited from AppTheme on purpose: when
      AppTheme is collapsed onto a DayNight base for 3.6.6, TV must not follow it.
    -->
    <style name="TvTheme" parent="Theme.MaterialComponents.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowFullscreen">true</item>
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/tv_window</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryVariant">@color/tv_window</item>
        <item name="colorOnPrimary">@color/tv_on_primary</item>
        <item name="android:windowDisablePreview">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
        <item name="colorSecondary">@color/tv_secondary</item>
        <item name="colorSecondaryVariant">@color/tv_secondary_variant</item>
        <item name="colorOnSecondary">@color/tv_on_secondary</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/tv_window</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The launch theme, and the only theme the system reads before the app runs.

      It exists because AppTheme carried android:windowDisablePreview="true",
      which does exactly what it says: no starting window on 24-30, and no
      platform splash on 31+. The measured cost was ~4.7s of untouched launcher
      after the tap on a cold start and ~6.8s after process death, with no
      acknowledgement of any kind - see docs/SPLASH-LAUNCH-AUDIT.md.

      Theme.SplashScreen back-ports the Android 12 splash, so these three items
      drive both paths from one place: the platform SplashScreen on 31+, and an
      equivalent starting window drawn from the same colour and icon on 24-30.
      Nothing here is a new design. The background is the `background` semantic
      role, already day/night and already what the app shell draws underneath
      every screen; the icon is ic_splash_mark, which is the app's own mark with
      the launcher plate taken off - see that file for what was removed and why.

      ic_launcher_foreground is deliberately NOT used here. It carries the yellow
      radial-gradient plate, which filled 63.5% of its viewport and rendered at
      184dp: a second branded splash screen rather than a bridge to one.

      postSplashScreenTheme hands the activity back to AppTheme the moment
      installSplashScreen() runs, before super.onCreate, so the live theme is
      AppTheme + AppTheme0..9 exactly as it was before this change. Only the
      window the system creates *before* the app has run is different.

      No setKeepOnScreenCondition anywhere: the splash is dismissed by the first
      frame the app draws, which is the whole point. Nothing waits for it.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Myata.Splash" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/background</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/ic_splash_mark</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      android:windowDisablePreview is gone; Theme.Myata.Splash above is why.
      windowIsTranslucent stays only because it is now inert: the system reads
      translucency from the activity's manifest theme, which is the splash theme,
      and a setTheme() after the window exists cannot make an opaque window
      translucent. Removing it is a separate cleanup, not part of this fix.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.Myata.Base">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <!--
          android:windowFullscreen is deliberately NOT set here, and its absence is
          load-bearing.

          It used to be true, which set FLAG_FULLSCREEN and hid the status bar. The
          consequence was not the missing clock - it was that every screen's
          `systemBars().top` inset became **zero**, because there was no status bar
          to inset for. Each screen already applies that inset correctly, so all of
          them faithfully applied nothing, and content landed at y=0: on a device
          with a hole-punch or a notch, Сейчас играет drew underneath the camera.

          targetSdk 36 makes this sharper rather than softer. The platform runs the
          window EDGE_TO_EDGE_ENFORCED with layoutInDisplayCutoutMode=always, so it
          is always allowed into the cutout; the only thing keeping content out of
          it is the inset the app applies. Hiding the status bar removed the one
          number that was doing that job.

          The frozen design assumes a status bar - its 16dp header offset is
          measured below one, and docs/PLAYER-3.6.6.md records the inset moving to
          the shell root for exactly that reason. Nothing in FINAL asks for
          fullscreen, so nothing here asks the platform for it.
        -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/main_fragment</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryVariant">@color/main_fragment</item>
        <item name="colorOnPrimary">@color/white</item>
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
        <item name="colorSecondary">@color/teal_200</item>
        <item name="colorSecondaryVariant">@color/teal_700</item>
        <item name="colorOnSecondary">@color/black</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/main_fragment</item>
    </style>
    <!--
      AppTheme0..9 are the ten random window backgrounds. MainActivity picks one
      per launch; this is product behaviour, not duplicate styles.

      screen0..9 live in res/drawable-nodpi/ and must stay there. An unqualified
      res/drawable/ does not mean "any density" - it means mdpi, so the framework
      scales the bitmap up by the device density on decode. At 1080x1921 on a
      420dpi device that decoded 2835x5043, 57MB per background, and the
      instrumented suite died with OutOfMemoryError before it could finish.
      nodpi means what drawable/ was mistaken for: decode as authored, 1080x1921,
      8.3MB, the same on every device.

      Nothing is lost by not scaling. A window background is drawn at the size of
      the window, not at its intrinsic size (BitmapDrawable gravity FILL), so the
      density scale never affected framing - only how many pixels were decoded
      before being stretched back. 1080 wide is exactly the width of the screens
      this app runs on, so the drawn result is unchanged.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme0" parent="Theme.Myata.Base">
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/purple_500</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryVariant">@color/main_fragment</item>
        <item name="colorOnPrimary">@color/white</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/screen0</item>
        <item name="colorSecondary">@color/teal_200</item>
        <item name="colorSecondaryVariant">@color/teal_700</item>
        <item name="colorOnSecondary">@color/black</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">?attr/colorPrimaryVariant</item>
    </style>
    <style name="AppTheme1" parent="Theme.Myata.Base">
